1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:01,959
Assalamu  alaikum  wa  rahmatullahi  wa

2
00:00:01,959 --> 00:00:03,159
barakatuh.

3
00:00:03,159 --> 00:00:05,080
Welcome  back  to  Prime.  In  the

4
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,560
first  lesson,  we  looked  at  why

5
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,480
project  management  matters  and  why

6
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,120
development  projects  need  more  than

7
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:12,360
good  intentions.  Now,  we  need  the

8
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:13,960
bigger  picture.

9
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:15,599
A  project  is  not  simply  a  list

10
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:17,240
of  activities  completed  one  after

11
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,080
another.  We  need  to  understand  the

12
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:21,039
need,  prepare  the  project,  plan

13
00:00:21,039 --> 00:00:23,279
the  work,  manage  implementation  and

14
00:00:23,279 --> 00:00:25,000
eventually  close  or  hand  over  the

15
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,439
project.  Project  DPro  gives  us  a

16
00:00:27,439 --> 00:00:29,519
structure  for  doing  this  alongside

17
00:00:29,519 --> 00:00:31,400
principle  and  decision  points  that

18
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:32,840
guide  how  we  think  throughout  the

19
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:33,879
journey.

20
00:00:33,879 --> 00:00:35,200
Each  phase  also  gives  us  a

21
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,800
question  to  answer  before  moving

22
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:37,416
ahead.

23
00:00:37,496 --> 00:00:39,896
Project  DPro  organizes  project

24
00:00:39,896 --> 00:00:41,576
management  into  five  connected

25
00:00:41,576 --> 00:00:42,735
phases.

26
00:00:42,735 --> 00:00:44,456
Identification  and  definition  is

27
00:00:44,456 --> 00:00:45,935
where  we  understand  the  need  and

28
00:00:45,935 --> 00:00:47,616
define  the  project.

29
00:00:47,616 --> 00:00:49,576
Project  setup  establishes  its

30
00:00:49,576 --> 00:00:50,856
authorization  and  management

31
00:00:50,856 --> 00:00:52,055
structure.

32
00:00:52,055 --> 00:00:53,496
Project  planning  turns  that

33
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:55,096
direction  into  an  implementation

34
00:00:55,096 --> 00:00:56,615
plan.

35
00:00:56,615 --> 00:00:58,256
Project  implementation  involves

36
00:00:58,256 --> 00:01:00,976
managing  work,  people,  resources,

37
00:01:00,976 --> 00:01:03,015
issues  and  changes.

38
00:01:03,015 --> 00:01:05,056
Finally,  project  closure  helps  the

39
00:01:05,056 --> 00:01:07,136
team  finish  responsibly,  including

40
00:01:07,136 --> 00:01:09,936
hand over,  reporting,  evaluation,

41
00:01:09,936 --> 00:01:12,336
and  learning.  These  phases  give  us

42
00:01:12,336 --> 00:01:14,207
a  map  for  the  decisions  ahead.

43
00:01:14,406 --> 00:01:16,647
These  phases  are  not  five  separate

44
00:01:16,647 --> 00:01:18,687
boxes.  We  do  not  always  finish

45
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:20,367
one,  forget  it  and  move  to  the

46
00:01:20,367 --> 00:01:21,566
next.

47
00:01:21,566 --> 00:01:23,607
Project  D-Pro  explains  that  they

48
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:25,047
interact.

49
00:01:25,047 --> 00:01:26,607
You  may  think  about  sustainability

50
00:01:26,607 --> 00:01:28,167
and  closure  while  designing  the

51
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:30,647
project.  During  implementation,  new

52
00:01:30,647 --> 00:01:32,646
information  may  make  you  revisit  a

53
00:01:32,646 --> 00:01:34,767
risk  or  change  the  plan.

54
00:01:34,767 --> 00:01:36,447
Planning  can  continue  as  better

55
00:01:36,447 --> 00:01:38,646
information  becomes  available.

56
00:01:38,646 --> 00:01:41,246
The  life  cycle  gives  direction,

57
00:01:41,246 --> 00:01:42,927
but  the  work  has  to  respond  to

58
00:01:42,927 --> 00:01:44,246
the  reality  of  the  project.

59
00:01:44,246 --> 00:01:45,207
object.

60
00:01:45,207 --> 00:01:46,687
That  is  why  the  information  from

61
00:01:46,687 --> 00:01:48,967
one  phase  must  remain  available  to

62
00:01:48,967 --> 00:01:50,847
the  team  in  another.

63
00:01:50,847 --> 00:01:52,727
A  plan  is  useful  only  while  it

64
00:01:52,727 --> 00:01:54,498
reflects  what  the  team  now  knows.

65
00:01:54,498 --> 00:01:56,498
The  phases  show  where  we  are  in  a

66
00:01:56,498 --> 00:01:58,658
project.  Project  D  Pro's  five

67
00:01:58,658 --> 00:02:00,578
principles  guide  how  we  manage  it

68
00:02:00,578 --> 00:02:02,858
in  every  phase.  Well-governed,

69
00:02:02,858 --> 00:02:05,137
participatory,  comprehensive,

70
00:02:05,137 --> 00:02:07,498
integrated,  and  adaptive.

71
00:02:07,498 --> 00:02:09,177
Think  of  these  as  habits,  not

72
00:02:09,177 --> 00:02:11,498
another  five  steps  to  complete.

73
00:02:11,498 --> 00:02:13,058
You  do  not  finish  being

74
00:02:13,058 --> 00:02:15,338
participatory  after  one  meeting.

75
00:02:15,338 --> 00:02:16,658
You  involve  the  right  people  when

76
00:02:16,658 --> 00:02:18,537
you  identify  a  need,  design  the

77
00:02:18,537 --> 00:02:20,617
work,  implement  activities,  and

78
00:02:20,617 --> 00:02:22,298
close  the  project.  The  same

79
00:02:22,298 --> 00:02:23,258
applies  to  the  other  four

80
00:02:23,258 --> 00:02:24,498
principles.

81
00:02:24,498 --> 00:02:26,097
The  principles  stay  relevant  even

82
00:02:26,097 --> 00:02:27,897
when  the  team  moves  from  analysis

83
00:02:27,897 --> 00:02:29,737
to  delivery.  They  shape  both  a

84
00:02:29,737 --> 00:02:31,698
small  choice  and  a  major  project

85
00:02:31,698 --> 00:02:32,456
decision.

86
00:02:32,456 --> 00:02:34,656
Well  governed  means  there  is

87
00:02:34,656 --> 00:02:36,735
clarity  about  decisions.  Who  has

88
00:02:36,735 --> 00:02:38,936
authority?  Who  is  accountable?  What

89
00:02:38,936 --> 00:02:40,576
can  the  project  manager  decide?

90
00:02:40,576 --> 00:02:42,536
And  when  must  a  decision  go  to  a

91
00:02:42,536 --> 00:02:44,656
sponsor  or  board?

92
00:02:44,656 --> 00:02:46,416
Without  that  clarity,  even  a  small

93
00:02:46,416 --> 00:02:48,856
change  can  cause  confusion.

94
00:02:48,856 --> 00:02:50,416
People  may  not  know  who  makes  the

95
00:02:50,416 --> 00:02:52,296
final  call  or  the  manager  may

96
00:02:52,296 --> 00:02:55,056
seek  approval  for  every  detail.

97
00:02:55,056 --> 00:02:55,895
Governance  sets  out

98
00:02:55,895 --> 00:02:57,936
responsibilities,  authority,

99
00:02:57,936 --> 00:03:00,256
tolerances  and  decision  making  so

100
00:03:00,256 --> 00:03:01,655
the  project  can  move  while

101
00:03:01,655 --> 00:03:03,176
remaining  accountable.

102
00:03:03,176 --> 00:03:04,616
If  an  issue  crosses  in  agreed

103
00:03:04,616 --> 00:03:06,535
tolerance,  the  manager  knows  when

104
00:03:06,535 --> 00:03:07,736
to  bring  it  to  the  right  decision

105
00:03:07,736 --> 00:03:08,456
makers.

106
00:03:08,736 --> 00:03:10,376
Participatory  means  giving  the

107
00:03:10,376 --> 00:03:12,095
right  stakeholders  meaningful

108
00:03:12,095 --> 00:03:14,376
opportunities  to  contribute.

109
00:03:14,376 --> 00:03:16,295
Development  projects  affect  people.

110
00:03:16,295 --> 00:03:17,336
They  should  not  be  designed

111
00:03:17,336 --> 00:03:19,536
entirely  from  an  office.

112
00:03:19,536 --> 00:03:21,175
Participation  can  improve  the

113
00:03:21,175 --> 00:03:23,175
information  we  collect,  strengthen

114
00:03:23,175 --> 00:03:25,575
design,  manage  expectations,  and

115
00:03:25,575 --> 00:03:28,096
build  ownership.  But  it  does  not

116
00:03:28,096 --> 00:03:29,895
mean  every  person  attends  every

117
00:03:29,895 --> 00:03:33,016
meeting  or  approves  every  decision.

118
00:03:33,016 --> 00:03:34,416
The  team  needs  to  consider  whose

119
00:03:34,416 --> 00:03:35,655
perspective  matters  for  a

120
00:03:35,655 --> 00:03:37,655
particular  choice  and  involve  them

121
00:03:37,655 --> 00:03:39,096
appropriately.

122
00:03:39,096 --> 00:03:40,816
We  will  explore  that  more  closely

123
00:03:40,816 --> 00:03:42,414
when  we  study  stakeholders.

124
00:03:42,574 --> 00:03:43,934
Two  principles  are  easy  to

125
00:03:43,934 --> 00:03:46,614
confuse.  Comprehensive  means

126
00:03:46,614 --> 00:03:48,654
considering  the  whole  project.

127
00:03:48,654 --> 00:03:50,654
Activities,  stakeholders,  risks,

128
00:03:50,654 --> 00:03:52,254
resources,  communication,

129
00:03:52,254 --> 00:03:54,013
sustainability,  monitoring,  and

130
00:03:54,013 --> 00:03:55,133
more.

131
00:03:55,133 --> 00:03:57,173
Integrated  means  these  parts  work

132
00:03:57,173 --> 00:03:58,414
together.

133
00:03:58,414 --> 00:04:00,894
A  schedule  affects  resources.  Risks

134
00:04:00,894 --> 00:04:02,374
may  affect  the  schedule.

135
00:04:02,374 --> 00:04:03,814
Stakeholder  decisions  can  affect

136
00:04:03,814 --> 00:04:06,054
scope.  Monitoring  may  show  that  a

137
00:04:06,054 --> 00:04:07,734
plan  needs  to  change.

138
00:04:07,734 --> 00:04:09,254
These  are  not  separate  documents

139
00:04:09,254 --> 00:04:11,294
prepared  only  to  fill  a  folder.

140
00:04:11,294 --> 00:04:12,853
They  are  connected  parts  of  one

141
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:15,334
project  and  one  set  of  decisions.

142
00:04:15,334 --> 00:04:17,254
In  the  Delta  River  case,  a  choice

143
00:04:17,254 --> 00:04:18,774
about  sanitation  would  affect

144
00:04:18,774 --> 00:04:21,133
community  engagement,  costs  and  the

145
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:22,455
way  success  is  measured.

146
00:04:22,575 --> 00:04:25,894
The  fifth  principle  is  adaptive.

147
00:04:25,894 --> 00:04:27,695
A  carefully  planned  project  will

148
00:04:27,695 --> 00:04:29,854
still  face  changes.  A  risk  may

149
00:04:29,854 --> 00:04:31,775
become  an  issue,  a  stakeholder

150
00:04:31,775 --> 00:04:34,054
need  may  shift,  or  an  assumption

151
00:04:34,054 --> 00:04:36,134
may  turn  out  to  be  wrong.

152
00:04:36,134 --> 00:04:37,815
Being  adaptive  does  not  mean

153
00:04:37,815 --> 00:04:39,415
changing  direction  whenever  someone

154
00:04:39,415 --> 00:04:42,015
has  a  new  idea.  It  means  using

155
00:04:42,015 --> 00:04:44,135
information  to  decide  whether

156
00:04:44,135 --> 00:04:46,535
change  is  needed,  then  managing  it

157
00:04:46,535 --> 00:04:48,455
in  a  controlled  way.

158
00:04:48,455 --> 00:04:50,574
Plans  and  other  project  documents

159
00:04:50,574 --> 00:04:52,974
can  be  updated  as  the  team  turns

160
00:04:52,974 --> 00:04:55,375
and  the  context  changes.

161
00:04:55,375 --> 00:04:57,255
A  team  may  keep  its  goal  while

162
00:04:57,255 --> 00:04:59,935
changing  a  method  or  schedule  when

163
00:04:59,935 --> 00:05:01,135
evidence  shows  the  original

164
00:05:01,135 --> 00:05:03,038
approach  is  no  longer  suitable.

165
00:05:03,038 --> 00:05:05,518
Project  Dpro  also  uses  decision

166
00:05:05,518 --> 00:05:07,078
gates,  points  where  the  right

167
00:05:07,078 --> 00:05:08,917
people  review  current  information

168
00:05:08,917 --> 00:05:10,678
and  ask,  "Are  we  still  doing  the

169
00:05:10,678 --> 00:05:12,998
right  project  in  the  right  way?"

170
00:05:12,998 --> 00:05:14,998
The  decision  might  be  to  continue,

171
00:05:14,998 --> 00:05:17,398
modify  the  project  or  stop  it.

172
00:05:17,398 --> 00:05:18,917
Stopping  is  not  automatically

173
00:05:18,917 --> 00:05:21,198
failure.  If  a  need  has  changed  or

174
00:05:21,198 --> 00:05:22,438
the  intervention  is  no  longer

175
00:05:22,438 --> 00:05:24,557
appropriate,  continuing  only  because

176
00:05:24,557 --> 00:05:25,958
work  is  already  started  could  make

177
00:05:25,958 --> 00:05:27,438
things  worse.

178
00:05:27,438 --> 00:05:28,637
A  gate  gives  the  project  a

179
00:05:28,637 --> 00:05:30,038
deliberate  moment  to  check  its

180
00:05:30,038 --> 00:05:31,317
direction.

181
00:05:31,317 --> 00:05:32,797
The  review  needs  current  evidence

182
00:05:32,797 --> 00:05:34,118
and  people  with  the  authority  to

183
00:05:34,118 --> 00:05:36,038
make  or  approve  the  next  decision.

184
00:05:36,278 --> 00:05:37,997
Here  is  governance  in  the  Delta

185
00:05:37,997 --> 00:05:40,397
River  Latrine  project.  Rain  is

186
00:05:40,397 --> 00:05:42,158
identified  as  a  risk  that  could

187
00:05:42,158 --> 00:05:44,118
delay  digging  the  holes.

188
00:05:44,118 --> 00:05:45,878
Imagine  that  it  rains  for  five

189
00:05:45,878 --> 00:05:47,438
straight  days.

190
00:05:47,438 --> 00:05:48,797
The  project  manager  can  make

191
00:05:48,797 --> 00:05:50,557
decisions  about  delays  of  up  to

192
00:05:50,557 --> 00:05:51,877
three  days.

193
00:05:51,877 --> 00:05:53,438
Five  days  is  beyond  that

194
00:05:53,438 --> 00:05:55,838
tolerance.  So  the  manager  escalates

195
00:05:55,838 --> 00:05:57,758
the  issue  to  the  project  board.

196
00:05:57,758 --> 00:05:59,397
The  board  reviews  the  situation

197
00:05:59,397 --> 00:06:01,358
and  the  proposed  response  then

198
00:06:01,358 --> 00:06:03,678
decides  how  to  proceed.

199
00:06:03,678 --> 00:06:05,358
The  example  shows  why  authority

200
00:06:05,358 --> 00:06:07,118
limits  matter  when  a  real  issue

201
00:06:07,118 --> 00:06:09,238
appears.  It  also  gives  the  manager

202
00:06:09,238 --> 00:06:10,917
a  clear  path  when  the  original

203
00:06:10,917 --> 00:06:13,121
response  is  beyond  their  authority.

204
00:06:13,121 --> 00:06:14,960
One  more  idea  runs  through  the

205
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:18,001
whole  journey.  Meal,  meaning

206
00:06:18,001 --> 00:06:19,801
monitoring,  evaluation,

207
00:06:19,801 --> 00:06:22,201
accountability,  and  learning.

208
00:06:22,201 --> 00:06:23,720
It  is  not  something  the  team  adds

209
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:26,161
at  the  end  to  write  a  report.

210
00:06:26,161 --> 00:06:28,000
Early  on,  evidence  helps  us

211
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,681
understand  the  need  and  decide

212
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:31,320
what  to  measure.

213
00:06:31,320 --> 00:06:33,240
During  implementation,  monitoring

214
00:06:33,240 --> 00:06:35,361
shows  progress  and  problems.

215
00:06:35,361 --> 00:06:36,640
Feedback  helps  us  remain

216
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,881
accountable  to  the  people  affected.

217
00:06:38,881 --> 00:06:40,881
Evaluation  and  learning  help  us

218
00:06:40,881 --> 00:06:42,240
judge  results  and  improve

219
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:43,880
decisions.

220
00:06:43,880 --> 00:06:45,680
Think  of  meal  as  information

221
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:47,481
flowing  through  the  project,

222
00:06:47,481 --> 00:06:50,041
helping  the  team  act  and  adapt.

223
00:06:50,041 --> 00:06:51,641
We  will  return  to  meal  as  each

224
00:06:51,641 --> 00:06:53,329
project  decision  takes  shape.

225
00:06:53,329 --> 00:06:55,769
Now,  you  have  the  map.  Five

226
00:06:55,769 --> 00:06:57,569
connected  phases  to  guide  where

227
00:06:57,569 --> 00:06:59,569
the  project  is  going,  five

228
00:06:59,569 --> 00:07:01,449
principles  to  guide  how  it  is

229
00:07:01,449 --> 00:07:04,169
managed,  decision  gates  to  check

230
00:07:04,169 --> 00:07:06,649
whether  to  continue,  and  meal  to

231
00:07:06,649 --> 00:07:08,809
support  decisions  with  evidence  and

232
00:07:08,809 --> 00:07:09,969
learning.

233
00:07:09,969 --> 00:07:12,089
Keep  the  Delta  River  in  mind.

234
00:07:12,089 --> 00:07:13,528
Before  a  team  commits  to  a

235
00:07:13,528 --> 00:07:15,129
solution,  what  does  it  need  to

236
00:07:15,129 --> 00:07:17,969
know  about  the  people,  the  river,

237
00:07:17,969 --> 00:07:19,889
and  the  available  services?

238
00:07:19,889 --> 00:07:21,809
That  question  takes  us  into  lesson

239
00:07:21,809 --> 00:07:24,249
three.  Understand  standing  needs

240
00:07:24,249 --> 00:07:26,969
and  collecting  useful  evidence.

241
00:07:26,969 --> 00:07:28,769
You  can  use  that  map  to  locate

242
00:07:28,769 --> 00:07:30,809
any  new  question  we  meet.

243
00:07:30,809 --> 00:07:32,449
That's  it  for  today.  I'll  see  you

244
00:07:32,449 --> 00:07:33,495
in  the  next  lesson.

